    summary: 'CQRS separates the write model (commands that change state) from the read model (queries that return data), allowing each to be optimized and scaled independently.',
    explanation: 'In a traditional CRUD model, the same data model handles both reads and writes, which can become a bottleneck when read and write patterns diverge significantly. CQRS splits these into a command side, which validates business rules and persists state changes, and a query side, which serves denormalized, read-optimized views often kept in a separate store like Elasticsearch or a materialized view. The two sides are typically synchronized asynchronously via events, meaning the read model is eventually consistent with the write model. CQRS pairs naturally with event sourcing but adds real complexity, so it is best reserved for subsystems with genuinely different read/write scaling needs, not applied wholesale to an entire application.',
    code: "// Command side: validates and mutates state\nclass PlaceOrderCommandHandler {\n  handle(cmd) {\n    const order = Order.create(cmd)\n    orderWriteRepo.save(order)\n    eventBus.publish('OrderPlaced', order.toEvent())\n  }\n}\n\n// Query side: denormalized read model, updated async\nclass OrderProjection {\n  on('OrderPlaced', e => {\n    readDb.upsert('order_summary', { id: e.orderId, status: 'placed' })\n  })\n}\n\n// Query handler reads only from the optimized read store\nfunction getOrderSummary(id) { return readDb.find('order_summary', id) }",
