    explanation: 'RabbitMQ implements the AMQP model where producers publish to exchanges that route messages to queues based on bindings, and once a consumer acknowledges a message it is typically removed from the queue, making it well suited to task distribution and complex routing. Kafka instead models messages as an append-only, partitioned, replicated log that retains events for a configurable period regardless of consumption, allowing multiple independent consumer groups to replay history at their own pace. Kafka is optimized for very high throughput and stream processing (e.g. millions of events/sec), while RabbitMQ shines for lower-latency, complex routing and per-message guarantees like priority queues. Choosing between them depends on whether you need log-based replay and stream processing (Kafka) or flexible routing and traditional work queues (RabbitMQ).',
