    explanation: 'Because distributed systems cannot use a single ACID transaction across service boundaries efficiently, sagas coordinate a sequence of local transactions where each service commits its own change and publishes an event or is invoked to trigger the next step. If a step fails partway through, previously completed steps are undone using compensating transactions (e.g. refund a payment that already succeeded) rather than a database rollback. Sagas can be orchestrated, where a central coordinator tells each service what to do and handles failures, or choreographed, where each service reacts to events from others with no central coordinator. Orchestration is easier to reason about and monitor, while choreography is more decoupled but can be harder to trace end-to-end.',
