    explanation: 'In cache-aside (lazy loading), the application checks the cache first, and on a miss reads from the database and populates the cache for next time, which is simple but can lead to stale data or a "thundering herd" on cache misses. Write-through caching writes to the cache and database synchronously on every update, keeping them consistent at the cost of write latency, while write-behind (write-back) writes to the cache immediately and asynchronously flushes to the database, improving write speed at the risk of data loss if the cache fails before flushing. Cache invalidation and appropriate TTLs are critical design decisions, since serving stale data can be worse than a cache miss. Distributed caches like Redis or Memcached are typically placed between the application and database tier, and can be layered further with CDN and browser caching at the edges.',
