    title: 'What is Database Sharding?',
    summary: 'Sharding horizontally partitions a database across multiple independent nodes, so each shard holds a subset of the data, allowing writes and storage to scale beyond a single machine.',
    explanation: 'Unlike replication, which copies the same data to multiple nodes, sharding splits data by a shard key (e.g. user ID range, hash, or geographic region) so each shard is responsible for a distinct portion of the dataset and no single node needs to store or serve all the data. This allows write throughput and storage capacity to scale roughly linearly with the number of shards, which vertical scaling or replication alone cannot achieve for write-heavy workloads. The tradeoffs are significant: cross-shard joins and transactions become expensive or impossible, rebalancing shards as data grows is operationally complex, and a poorly chosen shard key can create "hot shards" with uneven load. Common sharding strategies include range-based, hash-based, and directory-based (lookup service) sharding.',
