    title: 'What is the CAP Theorem?',
    summary: 'CAP theorem states that a distributed data store can only guarantee two of three properties at once during a network partition: Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance.',
    explanation: 'Consistency here means every read receives the most recent write or an error (linearizability), Availability means every request receives a non-error response (without guaranteeing it is the latest data), and Partition tolerance means the system continues to operate despite network failures between nodes. Since network partitions are unavoidable in real distributed systems, the practical choice is really between consistency and availability when a partition occurs (CP vs AP) — you cannot sacrifice partition tolerance and still call it a distributed system. CP systems like traditional consensus-based stores (e.g. etcd, Zookeeper) reject requests or block during a partition to preserve consistency, while AP systems like Cassandra or DynamoDB continue serving requests during a partition but may return stale data, resolving conflicts later. In practice, most real systems are more nuanced than a strict CP/AP label, and PACELC extends CAP by also considering the tradeoff between latency and consistency when there is no partition.',
    code: "// CP choice: reject write during partition to preserve consistency\nif (!quorumReachable()) {\n  throw new Error('Cannot guarantee consistency - rejecting write')\n}\n\n// AP choice: accept write during partition, resolve conflicts later\nif (!quorumReachable()) {\n  writeLocally(data)          // accept, stay available\n  queueForReconciliation(data) // resolve when partition heals\n}",
    interviewQuestion: 'Given that network partitions are inevitable, explain the real-world tradeoff CAP theorem forces between consistency and availability, and give an example of a system that chooses each.',
