    title: 'Why is idempotency important in distributed systems?',
    summary: 'An idempotent operation produces the same result no matter how many times it is executed, which is essential for safely retrying requests in unreliable distributed systems.',
    explanation: 'Networks are unreliable: a client may time out and retry a request even though the server actually processed it successfully, and without idempotency that retry could double-charge a customer or create duplicate records. Idempotency is typically achieved by having the client generate a unique idempotency key per logical operation, which the server stores alongside the result; if the same key arrives again, the server returns the cached result instead of reprocessing. HTTP methods like GET, PUT, and DELETE are naturally idempotent by specification, while POST is not, which is why payment and order-creation APIs commonly require an explicit idempotency key header. Idempotency is also foundational to safe message queue consumers, since at-least-once delivery guarantees mean the same message can be delivered more than once.',
    code: "// Idempotency key pattern for a payment API\napp.post('/payments', async (req, res) => {\n  const key = req.headers['idempotency-key']\n  const existing = await idempotencyStore.get(key)\n  if (existing) return res.json(existing)  // return cached result\n\n  const result = await chargeCard(req.body)\n  await idempotencyStore.set(key, result, { ttl: 86400 })\n  res.json(result)\n})",
    interviewQuestion: 'How would you design an idempotency key mechanism for a payments API to prevent double-charging on client retries?',
