    id: 'softwarearchitecture-rate-limiting',
    category: 'softwarearchitecture',
    difficulty: 'Advanced',
    topic: 'Resilience Patterns',
    title: 'What are common Rate Limiting architectures?',
    summary: 'Rate limiting restricts how many requests a client can make in a given time window, protecting services from overload or abuse, using algorithms like token bucket, leaky bucket, or sliding window.',
    explanation: 'The token bucket algorithm gives each client a bucket that refills at a fixed rate; each request consumes a token, and requests are rejected once the bucket is empty, which naturally allows short bursts up to the bucket size. The leaky bucket algorithm instead processes requests at a constant fixed rate regardless of burstiness, smoothing traffic but adding latency for bursts. Fixed window counters are simple but can allow up to 2x the limit at window boundaries, which sliding window log or sliding window counter algorithms address more precisely at the cost of more memory/computation. In distributed systems, rate limit state is typically stored in a shared fast store like Redis so limits are enforced consistently across multiple gateway or service instances rather than per-instance.',
    code: "// Token bucket rate limiter using Redis\nasync function allowRequest(userId, limit = 100, refillRatePerSec = 10) {\n  const key = `ratelimit:${userId}`\n  const bucket = await redis.get(key) || { tokens: limit, last: Date.now() }\n\n  const elapsed = (Date.now() - bucket.last) / 1000\n  bucket.tokens = Math.min(limit, bucket.tokens + elapsed * refillRatePerSec)\n  bucket.last = Date.now()\n\n  if (bucket.tokens < 1) { await redis.set(key, bucket); return false }\n  bucket.tokens -= 1\n  await redis.set(key, bucket)\n  return true\n}",
    interviewQuestion: 'Compare the token bucket and sliding window algorithms for rate limiting, and explain why fixed window counters can be gamed at window boundaries.',
