    explanation: 'A single general-purpose API often forces mobile clients to over-fetch data designed for web dashboards, or requires clunky query parameters to accommodate every consumer. The BFF pattern introduces a separate backend per frontend, each owned ideally by the same team as the client it serves, which calls downstream microservices and composes/shapes the response exactly as that client needs it, reducing round trips and payload size. This improves client performance and lets frontend teams iterate independently without coordinating changes to a shared general API, at the cost of some duplicated aggregation logic across BFFs. BFFs are especially popular when mobile and web clients have very different data and latency requirements, or when GraphQL gateways are used per client type instead of one universal schema.',
