    explanation: 'Named after the strangler fig vine that grows around a host tree and eventually replaces it, this pattern avoids risky "big bang" rewrites by placing a facade or routing layer in front of the legacy system that gradually redirects specific routes or features to newly built services while the rest continues to hit the legacy code. As more functionality is migrated and proven in production, the proportion routed to the new system grows until the legacy system handles nothing and can be retired. This dramatically reduces risk compared to a full rewrite, since each migrated slice can be tested and rolled back independently, and the team ships incremental value throughout the migration rather than a risky all-or-nothing cutover. The main challenge is maintaining the facade/router and handling data synchronization between old and new systems during the transition period.',
