    summary: 'Vertical scaling adds more resources (CPU/RAM) to a single machine, while horizontal scaling adds more machines that share the workload behind a load balancer.',
    explanation: 'Vertical scaling (scaling up) is simple since it requires no architectural changes, but it has a hard ceiling determined by the largest available machine, and it introduces a single point of failure since everything runs on one node. Horizontal scaling (scaling out) distributes load across many smaller machines, which offers near-limitless scaling potential and improved fault tolerance since the failure of one node does not take down the whole system, but it requires the application to be designed for statelessness, distributed session handling, and load balancing. Most modern cloud-native systems favor horizontal scaling for its resilience and elasticity, reserving vertical scaling for scenarios where the workload is inherently hard to parallelize, such as a single large in-memory computation or certain relational database write workloads. Auto-scaling groups in cloud platforms typically implement horizontal scaling by adding/removing instances based on load metrics like CPU or request count.',
    code: "// Vertical scaling: bigger machine, same architecture\n// t3.medium (2 vCPU, 4GB) -> t3.2xlarge (8 vCPU, 32GB)\n\n// Horizontal scaling: more machines behind a load balancer\nautoScalingGroup: {\n  minInstances: 2,\n  maxInstances: 10,\n  targetCpuUtilization: 60, // scale out when avg CPU > 60%\n  loadBalancer: 'app-lb',\n}\n// requires stateless app instances + shared session/cache store",
