    id: 'softwarearchitecture-fault-tolerance',
    category: 'softwarearchitecture',
    difficulty: 'Advanced',
    topic: 'Resilience Patterns',
    title: 'How do you design for Fault Tolerance in distributed systems?',
    summary: 'Fault tolerance is the ability of a system to continue operating correctly despite failures of its components, achieved through redundancy, isolation, and graceful degradation.',
    explanation: 'Redundancy means running multiple instances of each component (and replicas of data) so a single node failure does not cause an outage, often combined with health checks and automatic failover to redundant instances. Bulkheads isolate failures by partitioning resources (e.g. separate thread pools or connection pools per downstream dependency) so that a failure or slowdown in one dependency cannot exhaust resources needed by unrelated functionality, similar to watertight compartments in a ship. Graceful degradation means the system continues offering reduced functionality when a non-critical dependency fails (e.g. showing cached recommendations instead of failing the whole page if the recommendation service is down), rather than a hard failure. These techniques are typically combined with circuit breakers, timeouts, and chaos engineering practices (deliberately injecting failures, e.g. Netflix\'s Chaos Monkey) to continuously validate that the system tolerates real-world failures.',
    code: "// Bulkhead: isolate connection pools per dependency\nconst paymentPool = new ConnectionPool({ max: 10 })\nconst inventoryPool = new ConnectionPool({ max: 10 })\n// if payment service is slow, it can only exhaust its own 10 connections\n// -- inventory calls remain unaffected\n\n// Graceful degradation\nasync function getProductPage(id) {\n  const product = await productService.get(id) // critical, must succeed\n  let recommendations = []\n  try {\n    recommendations = await recommendationService.get(id) // non-critical\n  } catch (e) { /* degrade gracefully, show product without recs */ }\n  return { product, recommendations }\n}",
    interviewQuestion: 'What is the bulkhead pattern, and how does it prevent a failure in one dependency from cascading to unrelated parts of the system?',
