    summary: 'Observability is the ability to understand a system\'s internal state from its external outputs, built on three pillars: logs (discrete events), metrics (aggregated numeric measurements), and traces (request flow across services).',
    explanation: 'Logs are timestamped, discrete records of events (errors, state changes) useful for detailed debugging of a specific incident, but expensive to store and query at scale if unstructured. Metrics are numeric measurements aggregated over time (request rate, error rate, latency percentiles, CPU usage) that are cheap to store and great for dashboards, alerting, and spotting trends, but they lack the detail to explain "why" something happened. Distributed traces follow a single request as it propagates across multiple services, capturing timing and causality (via a shared trace ID and spans), which is essential for diagnosing latency and failures in microservices where a single user action can touch a dozen services. True observability combines all three, often via tools like Prometheus/Grafana for metrics, the ELK stack or Loki for logs, and Jaeger/Zipkin/OpenTelemetry for traces, correlated by shared identifiers like a trace ID or request ID.',
    code: "// OpenTelemetry-style trace span propagated across services\nconst span = tracer.startSpan('checkout.process', { traceId, parentSpanId })\ntry {\n  await paymentService.charge(order) // child span created inside\n  span.setStatus('OK')\n} catch (err) {\n  span.recordException(err)\n  span.setStatus('ERROR')\n} finally {\n  span.end()\n}\n\n// Correlated log line includes the same trace ID\nlogger.info('payment failed', { traceId, orderId, error: err.message })\n\n// Metric: increment counter for dashboards/alerts\nmetrics.increment('checkout.failures', { reason: 'payment_declined' })",
