    title: 'What are the tradeoffs between Multi-Tenant Architecture isolation models?',
    summary: 'Multi-tenant architecture serves multiple customers (tenants) from a shared application, with tenant isolation ranging from a fully shared database to fully separate databases per tenant.',
    explanation: 'In the shared database, shared schema model, all tenants\' rows live in the same tables distinguished by a tenant_id column, which is the most cost-efficient and simplest to operate but carries the highest risk of data leakage if a query forgets the tenant filter, and "noisy neighbor" tenants can degrade performance for others. The silo model gives each tenant a fully separate database (or even separate infrastructure), maximizing isolation, security, and the ability to customize per tenant, but it is the most expensive to operate and scale to thousands of tenants, and cross-tenant analytics become harder. The bridge model, shared database with separate schemas per tenant, is a middle ground offering better isolation than shared-schema without the full operational cost of silos. Real systems often use a hybrid: most tenants share infrastructure (pool model) while large enterprise customers with strict compliance needs get dedicated siloed instances, and row-level security or middleware-enforced tenant scoping is critical regardless of model to prevent cross-tenant data leaks.',
    code: "// Shared schema: every query must scope by tenant_id\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE tenant_id = ? AND status = 'pending'\n// risk: forgetting the WHERE clause leaks data across tenants\n\n// Enforce at the ORM/middleware level, not per-query discipline\nfunction scopedQuery(tenantId) {\n  return db('orders').where({ tenant_id: tenantId }) // always injected\n}\n\n// Silo model: tenant routed to its own DB connection\nconst tenantDb = getDbConnectionForTenant(tenantId)\nawait tenantDb('orders').where({ status: 'pending' })",
